// =============================================================================
// Arcet Political Ontology — Prisma schema
// =============================================================================
// Design decisions (read before editing):
//
// 1. One base `Entity` table provides identity, search, permissions and graph
//    compatibility for every political object. Typed *Profile tables hold the
//    type-specific fields and link 1:1 to Entity via a unique `entityId`.
//
// 2. The graph is relational-as-graph. Edges (`Relationship`) and "soft pointers"
//    (e.g. `primaryOrganisationId`, `partyEntityId`, `currentRepresentativeEntityId`)
//    are stored as indexed UUID columns and resolved in the service layer rather
//    than as formal Prisma relations. This is deliberate: formalising every
//    cross-entity reference against the single `Entity` table produces an
//    explosion of named back-relations and makes the schema unreadable. Every
//    such column is documented and indexed. Referential integrity for soft
//    pointers is enforced in the service layer.
//
// 3. Formal Prisma relations are used only where they are structurally
//    unambiguous: profile<->entity (1:1), and the pure join/child tables
//    (EntityEvidence, RelationshipEvidence, EventParticipant, RiskLink,
//    ComplianceFlag).
//
// 4. Field and enum names follow the coding brief verbatim so downstream
//    modules (FPTP simulator, candidate selection) bind to the same identifiers.
// =============================================================================

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

